speechtext:
I will read the resolution which the letter encloses:
Extract from minutes of annual meeting of the Eastern Townships Associated Boards of Trade, held at Sherbooke, March 16, 1915:
It was moved by J. A. Watson, seconded by Geo. W. A. Reburn, and resolved:
That whereas the Eastern Townships Associated Boards of Trade have learned from the delegates to this meeting from Farmers' Clubs that the production of our farmers cannot well be increased until the price of feed for our cattle and food for our population be reduced to within a measurcable distance of where these prices were before the outbreak of hos. tilities;
Whereas the milling companies are obliging dealers in the Eastern Townships to accept, in each shipment of feed, many bags of flour which they do not actually require, at the excessive, increased prevailing price, which is unreasonable when the present price of wheat is considered, thus loading ordinary dealers with high-priced flour ;
Whereas it is evident that the present price of grain is not increased sufficiently to warrant the excessive and increased price now charged for flour, bran, shorts, etc., by the milling companies of the Dominion ;
Whereas this is certainly done in restraint of trade;
Now, therefore, be it resolved that these Eastern Townships Associated Boards of Trade protest against such increased price of food stuffs, in so far as this increase is not absolutely justifiable;
That this protest be rendered effective by ' forwarding copy of this resolution to the Minister of Trade and Commerce, to the Minister of Agriculture, and to the members of Parliament representing the Eastern Townships at Ottawa, with a request that it be handed for action to the commission which has already been appointed to investigate the high cost of living.
And furthermore that these Eastern Townships Associated Boards of Trade severely deprecate the idea of any persons or companies using present war conditions to enrich themselves.
Certified true copy.
W. M. Hillhouse, Acting Secretary.
I hope the minister will take this matter into his consideration, and lay the letter and resolution before the High Cost of Living Commission.
